(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction is stabilized and taken to the catheterization laboratory. Dye shows a tight narrowing of the anterior interventricular artery, which is opened with a balloon and held open with a drug-eluting stent. Which term names the procedure that opened the artery?","choices":["Plain old balloon angioplasty","Left heart catheterization","Percutaneous coronary intervention","Coronary angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Percutaneous coronary intervention is the placement of a drug-eluting or non-drug-eluting stent to keep the coronary artery open. Anything done to the vessel during the catheterization, beyond imaging it, is the intervention. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"What is the normal relationship between leg and arm systolic pressure?","choices":["The relationship varies unpredictably and cannot be used","They are normally identical","Arm systolic is normally higher than leg","Leg systolic is normally higher than arm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so a leg pressure that is not higher is the abnormal finding. Leg pressures are measured when the pulse examination suggests coarctation. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does the body respond to increased metabolic demand in heart failure?","choices":["Slows heart rate to lengthen filling time","Lowers contractility to spare oxygen","Raises heart rate, contractility and preload","Lowers preload through venous pooling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neurohormonal input raises the heart rate, catecholamines and autonomic input raise contractility, and venous capacitance and renal compensation augment preload. These help at first and later drive damage. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has a mid-systolic click at the apex. An echocardiogram is ordered. Which finding confirms the suspected valve problem?","choices":["A dilated tricuspid annulus","A dilated aortic root","Thick, redundant mitral leaflets","A calcified, narrowed aortic valve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is diagnosed by echocardiogram showing thickened, redundant mitral leaflets greater than 5 mm, flaring into the left atrium during systole, from myxomatous degeneration. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with severe tricuspid stenosis develops worsening hepatic congestion, and her team fears progression to cirrhosis. Which intervention is reserved for this situation?","choices":["Afterload reduction therapy only","Mitral valve clip placement","Transcatheter aortic valve replacement","Balloon valvotomy or valve repair"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Balloon valvotomy and valve repair are reserved for tricuspid stenosis with hepatic congestion that may lead to cirrhosis, or with severe systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has two days of breathlessness after a viral illness. His N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is 90. How should the result be read?","choices":["The result is indeterminate","Heart failure is likely","Acute heart failure is likely","Acute heart failure is ruled out"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An N-terminal fragment below 300 rules out acute heart failure with a negative predictive value above 90%, and 90 is also below the under-75 heart failure threshold of 125. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man in the intensive care unit with acute heart failure has cool extremities and hypotension, and inotropic support is being considered. Which agents are used for this?","choices":["Dobutamine or milrinone","Spironolactone or eplerenone","Diltiazem or verapamil","Losartan or irbesartan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inotropic therapy for intensive care use includes the sympathomimetics dobutamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine and the phosphodiesterase inhibitor milrinone. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How is droxidopa usually used?","choices":["As sole first-line therapy for fainting","As an emergency infusion in intensive care","As an adjunct to midodrine or fludrocortisone","As a substitute for trigger avoidance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Droxidopa has only mild efficacy, so it is usually added to midodrine or fludrocortisone, and it is the better choice when supine hypertension is present. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 68-year-old smoker with peripheral arterial disease has blood pressure of 168/94 despite three agents. An abdominal bruit is audible, ultrasound shows a right kidney 2 cm smaller than the left, and he has had two episodes of sudden pulmonary edema. What is the most likely underlying cause?","choices":["Obstructive sleep apnea","Chronic kidney disease from diabetes","Renovascular disease","Primary aldosteronism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cluster is characteristic: resistant pressure, an abdominal bruit, asymmetric kidneys and recurrent flash pulmonary edema. In an older patient with established vascular disease the mechanism is atherosclerotic stenosis, often bilateral and progressive. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy is brought for a routine visit. His father had a heart attack at 40 and was found to have very high cholesterol. What is the most appropriate step regarding the boy's lipids?","choices":["Elective lipid screening now","Start a statin without testing","Wait until age 9 to 11","Screen only after puberty"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Elective screening is appropriate for patients as young as 2 years old with a family history of heart disease or high cholesterol. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl has palpitations and dizziness on standing. Within 10 minutes of standing her heart rate rises from 70 to 114, and her pressure does not fall. Does this meet the heart rate criterion for her age?","choices":["Yes; any rise over 20 per minute","No; adolescents need a rise of 50","No; the rise must occur in 3 minutes","Yes; the rise exceeds 40 per minute"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In adolescents the threshold is an increase of 40 beats per minute; her rise of 44 within 10 minutes meets it. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man on an angiotensin receptor blocker and a dihydropyridine remains above goal at maximally tolerated doses. What is the next step?","choices":["Add an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","Add spironolactone now","Add a thiazide-type diuretic","Add a second dihydropyridine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The foundational three are a renin-angiotensin blocker, a dihydropyridine and a thiazide-type diuretic, all titrated before resistance can be considered confirmed. It is the third of the foundational agents. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has sudden severe breathlessness, crackles and edema. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 1,150. How should this level be read?","choices":["Only chronic heart failure fits","The result is indeterminate","Acute heart failure is likely","Acute heart failure is ruled out"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Above 900 acute heart failure is likely. Above 400 heart failure is likely in general, 100 to 400 is indeterminate, and below 100 rules acute failure out. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with hypertension has never had cardiac symptoms. An echocardiogram shows left ventricular hypertrophy with preserved function. Which heart failure stage is he in?","choices":["Stage A","Stage B","Stage C","Stage D"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic structural heart disease, including left ventricular hypertrophy, is stage B, pre-heart failure. Stage A requires a structurally normal heart. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which diastolic change on standing meets the orthostatic criterion?","choices":["A fall of more than 10 mmHg within 10 minutes","A fall of more than 30 mmHg within 3 minutes","A fall of more than 20 mmHg within 3 minutes","A fall of more than 10 mmHg within 3 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A diastolic fall of more than 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing meets the orthostatic criterion alongside the systolic thresholds. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with episodic headache, sweating, palpitations and pallor has raised plasma free metanephrines and an adrenal mass. He is scheduled for resection. What must be established before surgery?","choices":["Alpha blockade first, then beta blockade","Neither, provided the tumor is resected promptly","Beta blockade first, then alpha blockade","Either order, provided both are established before surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the order is not negotiable: isolated beta blockade leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can precipitate a hypertensive crisis. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with long-standing pulmonary hypertension and a dilated right ventricle develops a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which process most likely produced her valve leak?","choices":["Endocarditis of the leaflets","Dilation of the tricuspid annulus","Rheumatic leaflet narrowing","A prior valvuloplasty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The most common cause of tricuspid regurgitation is dilation of the tricuspid annulus from right ventricular dilation in the setting of pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure and congestion is started on furosemide. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["No blood tests are needed on this drug","It will raise his potassium to high levels","His liver enzymes will be checked weekly","Potassium and kidney labs will be checked"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Furosemide is a potassium-wasting loop diuretic, so potassium and renal function are followed on the complete metabolic panel. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a prior heart attack and symptomatic peripheral arterial disease takes a maximal statin, and his low-density lipoprotein is 82 mg/dL. Which change is inappropriate at this point?","choices":["Rechecking lipids after the change","Adding evolocumab before ezetimibe","Continuing the maximal statin","Adding ezetimibe to the statin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If the injectable class is considered, ezetimibe is added to the maximal statin before it; skipping that step is out of sequence and less cost-effective. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 48-year-old Black man develops lip and tongue swelling three months after starting an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. What should be known about this?","choices":["An angiotensin receptor blocker carries no risk whatsoever","A beta blocker is the appropriate substitution","It is a dose-related effect that resolves on dose reduction","It is two to four times more common in this group"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a bradykinin-mediated class effect of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition. Angiotensin receptor blockers can usually be used instead, though angioedema with them remains possible. An angiotensin receptor blocker is usually substituted. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a known aortic dissection develops sudden aortic regurgitation with cardiogenic shock and pulmonary edema. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Emergency valve replacement or repair","Percutaneous balloon valve dilation","Outpatient afterload reduction therapy","Repeat echocardiogram in six months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute severe aortic regurgitation is treated with emergency aortic valve replacement or repair, ideally within 24 hours. Acute causes include myocardial infarction, endocarditis, aortic dissection and trauma. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 31, 26"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with severe pulmonic stenosis awaiting valvuloplasty has ankle swelling and an enlarged liver from right heart failure. Which medication relieves these symptoms?","choices":["Nitroprusside","Warfarin","A nitrate","A diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In severe symptomatic pulmonic stenosis, diuretics give symptomatic relief of right heart failure, alongside pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty for the valve itself. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has chest tightness the morning after a long-distance run that left his leg muscles sore. The team wants a blood marker specific to heart muscle. Which marker should be measured?","choices":["Smooth-muscle troponin","Troponin C","Any troponin type","Troponin I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Troponins are proteins of cardiac and skeletal muscle, and there are three types (I, T and C), but troponin I is the one specific to the heart, so skeletal muscle injury from his run does not confound it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has recurrent faints with a prodrome, but after a careful history the source is still uncertain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin midodrine empirically","Refer to the right specialty","Tell her the faints are harmless","Start a vasopressor at home"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of diagnosing reflex syncope, or of referring to the right specialty for confirmation when it stays uncertain. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which drug is avoided in a right ventricular infarct?","choices":["Clopidogrel","Aspirin","Nitroglycerin","Heparin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a right ventricular infarct nitroglycerin is not given: lowering preload removes the Frank-Starling support the failing right ventricle depends on, dropping output and precipitating shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes fainted after walking to the bathroom. Supine she is 146/84 with a heart rate of 70; after 3 minutes standing she is 118/70 with a heart rate of 72. What does her heart rate response indicate?","choices":["An arrhythmia is present","The autonomic response has failed","Volume depletion is the cause","The autonomic response is intact"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pressure fall with only a minimal heart rate rise means the sympathetic reflex is not compensating, which favors neurogenic orthostatic hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has right-sided heart failure confirmed by echocardiogram. Her team now wants to know how hemodynamically severe it is. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Troponin trend over a day","Chest radiograph series","Repeat echocardiogram","Right heart catheterization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once right-sided failure is established, right heart catheterization measures its hemodynamic severity. The echocardiogram has already made the diagnosis. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man hospitalized with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction asks why a defibrillator is being discussed. Which cause accounts for about half of cardiac arrests in hospitalized patients like him?","choices":["Ventricular arrhythmias","Complete heart block","Low potassium alone","Acute pulmonary embolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. About 52% of cardiac arrests in hospitalized patients with reduced ejection fraction are due to ventricular arrhythmias, which is the reason for defibrillator protection in those who meet the criteria. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with microvascular angina asks which factor tends to go along with her episodes. Her coronary arteries were normal on angiography. What should she be told?","choices":["Cocaine or amphetamine use","The hours after midnight","Stress or anxiety","Triptan migraine therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina is more frequent in women and is often associated with stress or anxiety. Proposed mechanisms include endothelial dysfunction, insulin resistance and estrogen deficiency. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 94, 95"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with untreated severe aortic stenosis now has breathlessness on exertion, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and ankle swelling. Without valve replacement, which average survival is expected from this symptom?","choices":["About 2 years","About 10 years","About 5 years","About 3 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Of the cardinal symptoms, heart failure or exertional dyspnea is the most common and carries the worst prognosis, about 2 years. Angina carries about 5 years and syncope about 3 years. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 16, 22"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has episodes of jaw and tooth pain when she walks uphill that ease when she rests. Her dentist found no dental cause. Which is the most likely cause of her pain?","choices":["Pancreatitis","Aortic dissection","Gallbladder disease","Myocardial ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exertional pain relieved by rest is ischemic, and acute coronary syndrome refers pain most commonly to the jaw, tooth or arm. Toothache is even listed among the descriptors of angina. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 31"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has low-density lipoprotein of 290 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride. She has firm nodules along both Achilles tendons (tendon xanthomas), and her father had a heart attack at 48. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Mixed dyslipidemia","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein of 200 to 400 mg/dL, normal triglyceride, Achilles tendon xanthomas (present in about 75%) and premature coronary disease in a first-degree relative fit one mutant allele. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 61-year-old with abrupt severe interscapular pain has symmetric, normal-volume pulses in all four limbs. What follows?","choices":["Dissection is excluded and another cause should be sought","An aortic regurgitation murmur would be required to proceed","Normal pulses do not exclude dissection","A normal chest radiograph would then exclude it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Absent pulse findings specifically do not exclude the diagnosis, which is why a normal pulse examination must not close the question. Imaging is still driven by the clinical suspicion. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic left-sided heart failure develops right-sided failure. He asks what this means for his outlook. Which reply is accurate?","choices":["His prognosis is now much worse","It improves once edema clears","His prognosis is now better","His prognosis is unchanged"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Right-sided failure carries a much worse prognosis than left-sided failure, and in him it marks progression of chronic left-sided disease. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man, three days after an acute myocardial infarction, has sudden breathlessness, crackles in both lungs and a new holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Tricuspid annular dilation","Papillary muscle rupture","Myxomatous degeneration","Rheumatic heart disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An acute myocardial infarction with papillary muscle rupture is an acute cause of mitral regurgitation, and acute mitral regurgitation presents with left-sided heart failure symptoms such as dyspnea and rales. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man is ready for discharge after an ST-elevation myocardial infarction. His heart rate is 78/min with no bradycardia. Which drug class improves survival rates across the board after infarction?","choices":["Glycoprotein 2b/3a inhibitor","Beta blocker","Calcium channel blocker","Sublingual nitroglycerin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers improve survival rates across the board after myocardial infarction, so they belong in post-infarction management unless bradycardia or another contraindication is present. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with orthostatic hypotension keeps standing up quickly despite being told not to, because he does not see why it matters. Which approach most improves his adherence?","choices":["Explain why the pressure falls","Add a vasopressor at home","Repeat the same instruction","Stop discussing it with him"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Understanding is the key to compliance: once a patient knows why the pressure falls on standing, slow position changes make sense and are followed. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which heart sound belongs to diastolic heart failure?","choices":["An opening snap","A pericardial rub","Third heart sound","Fourth heart sound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A fourth heart sound is strong left atrial contraction against a stiff, hypertrophied left ventricle, and it sits in the diastolic chain with hypertension and edema that starts peripherally. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with heart failure has cool, clammy extremities, a narrow pulse pressure and sleepiness, along with orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and leg edema. Which bedside hemodynamic profile fits?","choices":["Cold and wet","Warm and wet","Cold and dry","Warm and dry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-perfusion signs (cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure, obtundation) plus congestion signs (orthopnea, edema) place her in the cold and wet profile, hypoperfusion with congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which test shows a regional wall motion abnormality?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Calcium score","Echocardiogram","Troponin I level"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Regional wall motion abnormalities (hypokinesia, akinesia, dyskinesia) are seen on an echocardiogram, which images the moving walls of the ventricle segment by segment. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 35"},{"q":"A 14-year-old girl with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia remains severely elevated despite a high-intensity statin, ezetimibe and regular apheresis. Which adjunct is approved at her age?","choices":["Evinacumab","Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil","Fish oil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Evinacumab, an intravenous monoclonal antibody that lowers low-density lipoprotein and triglyceride, is adjunct therapy for patients 12 years and older with the homozygous disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Which drug is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist for a mechanical valve?","choices":["Heparin","Warfarin","Aspirin","Clopidogrel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Warfarin is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist, the long-term anticoagulant for mechanical heart valves; if surgery requires heparin instead, warfarin is bridged until the ratio is therapeutic. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which drug combinations are preferred as initial therapy?","choices":["A beta blocker with a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker","Two thiazide-type diuretics together","A renin-angiotensin blocker with a calcium blocker or diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the classes act by complementary mechanisms. A single-pill combination is preferred where two agents are started, since it improves adherence. That means an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker paired with a dihydropyridine or a thiazide-type diuretic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after taking a large quantity of a relative's blood pressure pills. His pressure dropped suddenly over the past hour and he is dizzy. How is his hypotension best classified?","choices":["Long-lasting acute infectious hypotension","Chronic hypotension from a cardiac cause","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Acute hypotension from a toxic cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Medication-mediated overdose is a toxic cause of acute hypotension, which is of sudden onset, usually symptomatic and tied to a specific trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 27%) has blood pressure above goal on her heart failure regimen. Verapamil is proposed. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Avoid it and use recommended classes","Use it in place of her beta blocker","Start it as first-line pressure control","Add it once she reaches class IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker, and calcium channel blockers are avoided in heart failure. Pressure control comes from optimizing the recommended classes as tolerated. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man is found to have a blood pressure of 158/92 in both arms. His femoral pulses are weak and delayed relative to the radial pulses, and his leg systolic pressure is lower than his arm pressure. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Primary hypertension","Coarctation of the aorta","Primary aldosteronism","Renovascular disease from fibromuscular dysplasia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leg systolic normally EXCEEDS arm systolic, so a reversal is the abnormal finding, and radial-femoral delay places a structural obstruction between the two territories. Early onset hypertension is itself a clue to a secondary cause. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is the treatment of choice in homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia?","choices":["Low-density lipoprotein apheresis","Lifestyle and dietary change alone","Fibrate monotherapy with diet","Moderate-intensity statin alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Apheresis, like dialysis, is the elective removal of low-density particles from the circulation; most patients also need more than one drug, starting with high-intensity statins. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis is in sinus rhythm. Which electrocardiogram finding is most expected?","choices":["Right axis deviation alone","Left atrial abnormality","Right atrial enlargement only","An expected normal tracing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis raises left atrial size and pressure, so the electrocardiogram shows left atrial abnormality. Atrial fibrillation is common in severe disease, and right ventricular hypertrophy appears with pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman with dyslipidemia weighs 200 pounds and asks how much weight she should aim to lose. Which goal is most appropriate?","choices":["About 60 to 80 pounds","No weight loss is needed","About 10 to 20 pounds","About 1 to 2 pounds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Therapeutic lifestyle changes aim for a 5% to 10% reduction of initial weight; for 200 pounds that is 10 to 20 pounds. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman without diabetes has a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 11% and an uncertain statin decision. She has never smoked and has no family history of premature disease. Her coronary artery calcium score is zero. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Withhold or delay the statin","Add ezetimibe now","Start a high-intensity statin","Order a skin biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A calcium score of zero lowers risk, so statin therapy may be withheld or delayed, except in cigarette smokers, diabetes or a strong family history of premature disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has low-density lipoprotein of 178 mg/dL. He reports fatigue, weight gain and cold intolerance over the past year. Before a statin is started, which test should be ordered?","choices":["Fasting triglyceride only","Thyroid function tests","Coronary artery calcium score","Skin biopsy for receptor activity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Decreased thyroid hormone down-regulates hepatic low-density lipoprotein receptors and raises low-density lipoprotein, so elevated levels call for screening for low thyroid before a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has diabetic autonomic neuropathy and also congestive heart failure. He faints on standing. Which of the following should be avoided?","choices":["Treating the underlying cause","Slow position changes","Fludrocortisone","A careful history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fludrocortisone causes salt and water retention and is avoided in congestive heart failure, even though it is best with the tachycardia syndrome. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the Graham Steell murmur?","choices":["Tricuspid regurgitation's inspiratory murmur","Pulmonic regurgitation's early diastolic murmur","Aortic stenosis's harsh systolic murmur","Tricuspid stenosis's mid-diastolic rumble"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Graham Steell murmur is the brief, high-pitched, early diastolic decrescendo murmur of pulmonic regurgitation at the left second intercostal space, radiating to the mid-right sternal border with full inspiration. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has asymptomatic left ventricular hypertrophy on echocardiogram. She already takes an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. Which drug class should be added for her diabetes-related risk?","choices":["Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Calcium channel blocker such as amlodipine","Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. She is stage B, and stage B carries a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for patients with diabetes as well as the converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which family history counts as premature coronary heart disease?","choices":["A male first-degree relative before 65","Any second-degree relative before 70","A female first-degree relative before 55","A male first-degree relative before 55"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Premature coronary heart disease means disease in a male first-degree relative before 55 or a female first-degree relative before 65. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman presents with fatigue, breathlessness with exertion and new ankle edema. Which blood test best supports a clinical diagnosis of heart failure?","choices":["Complete blood count","B-type natriuretic peptide","Serum potassium","Cardiac troponin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is a clinical diagnosis: signs and symptoms plus an elevated natriuretic peptide or objective congestion. The peptide is the laboratory test that carries that weight. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had his diuretic dose increased 10 days ago. He now has lightheadedness on standing, a 28 mmHg systolic fall and a heart rate rise from 70 to 94. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Reduce the recently increased diuretic","Start fludrocortisone","Start midodrine for the pressure","Add droxidopa as an adjunct"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The offending agent is removed first: iatrogenic hypotension is treated by reducing or removing the medication, with slow position changes meanwhile. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has had chest pressure at rest for two days. His electrocardiogram shows inverted T waves, and two standard troponin I levels are 0.01 ng/mL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["ST-elevation infarction","Infarction without ST elevation","Unstable angina","Stable angina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pain at rest with nonspecific electrocardiogram changes, usually inverted T waves, and a normal troponin I is unstable angina. A standard troponin I at or below 0.04 ng/mL is not abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with exertional chest pressure has a nuclear stress test that is abnormal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat the stress test","Cardiac catheterization","Discharge with no follow-up","Coronary calcium score"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An abnormal nuclear stress test leads to cardiac catheterization, where coronary angiography, the gold standard for diagnosing coronary artery disease, defines the lesions. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 38"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man remains at 158/96 on three agents at maximally tolerated doses. What is the commonest explanation to exclude first?","choices":["Undiagnosed primary aldosteronism","Medication nonadherence","An incorrectly sized cuff","Progressive renal artery stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it heads a list that also includes measurement error, white coat effect, high sodium or alcohol intake, interfering drugs, and undiagnosed sleep apnea or secondary hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
